raw:
Introduction Failure of tissues and organs due to damage, congenital disabilities, and aging is becoming the main issue of human body health all over the world. Bones are one of the strongest and primary tissues of the body, maintaining the surrounding soft tissues 1 . Regarding synthetic scaffolds that promote bone tissue repair, the dual role of bone tissue engineering (BTE) scaffolds in interacting with the extracellular matrix and providing load-bearing capacity is a challenge. This research aims to investigate the use and processing of several materials and hierarchical architectures to simulate the structure of natural bone tissues so that they may function as bioactive artificial scaffolds 2 . It is widely believed that designing and preparing new tissues is one of the most effective and efficient ways that tissue engineering can accelerate bone regeneration during the healing process 3 . A new strategy for repairing bones has emerged that is based on tissue engineering. As a result of its use of biomaterial scaffolds along with cells and growth factors, it can induce the formation of new bone tissues while reducing the risks associated with autologous bone grafts 4 . Bone tissue is a composite of approximately 70% nano-hydroxyapatite and 30% collagen. Wet collagen and the accumulation of mineral crystals penetrate each other and form a mineral fiber on a scale of several hundred nanometers. The primary role of bone is to provide a structural framework and mechanical strength, regulate blood pH, and maintain calcium and phosphate levels for metabolic processes 5 . It is desirable to design a biocompatible composite that can mimic the natural extracellular bone matrix and accelerate new bone regeneration 6 . If the dispersed phase used in the composite is nanoparticles, the composite material will be a nanocomposite. Natural bone is an example of a nanocomposite composed of two major phases, organic and inorganic, at the nanoscale 7 . Polymer-based nanocomposites are attractive due to their ability to create porous architectures and biodegradability. The ease of manipulating structures with precise tuning of physicochemical properties is an advantage of polymer nanocomposites for tissue engineering applications such as dental, bone, and neural engineering 8 . Nanocomposites made of polymeric materials and additives are potential materials for constructing bone scaffolds 9 . Polymers for making hydrogels include natural and synthetic polymers. Natural polymers are mainly natural components that prepare scaffolds with high biocompatibility, biodegradability, and natural cell adhesion. Recently, synthetic polymers have been widely used to make bone tissue engineering scaffolds because they have high mechanical properties and long life. Carboxymethyl cellulose is a biocompatible and biodegradable anionic polymer that can create a strong ionic cross-link in the scaffold 10  and increase the biochemical characteristics of porosity, swelling, and degradation. Cellulose is mainly used for various biomedical applications 11 . Among natural polysaccharides, alginate biopolymer is also widely used as a biomaterial for bone tissue engineering. Alginate is one of the most well-known substances, and it has the property of forming a suitable scaffold that can be useful for the treatment of organ failure and loss. Alginate consists of guluronic acid and mannuronic acid and is used in tissue engineering due to its biocompatibility, non-toxicity, abundance, and low price 12 . Alginate hydrogels can increase minimal protein absorption due to their hydrophilic property 13 . Sodium alginate is anionic and can be dissolved in water. Hydroxyl groups can cause strong interactions between sodium alginate and other polymers through hydrogen bonding 14 . Alginate benefits osteoblast growth due to its functional groups, such as hydroxyl and carboxyl, and increases the efficiency of implant substrates. Due to its high degradation rate in cell culture, its use in biomedical applications is limited. To improve alginate’s biocompatibility and mechanical properties, its combination with synthetic polymers and clay minerals was investigated 15 . Given the disadvantages of natural polymers, such as poor solubility in common solvents, their low mechanical properties, and poor stability in physiological environments, There have been numerous studies conducted on the physicochemical properties of composite polymers, which have proven to be effective in overcoming the disadvantages of natural polymers 16 . Blending polymers is a potential method for improving mechanical flexibility 17 . Polyvinyl alcohol is a hydrophilic semi-crystalline synthetic biopolymer containing many hydroxyl groups, and it can also form hydrogen bonds with free water molecules 18 . Polyvinyl alcohol is biocompatible and has high mechanical strength, low toxicity, large surface area, and good chemical stability; in a clinical setting, its use is possible to construct membranes, repair tissues, and construct biomaterials 16 . By synergistically combining the bioactive properties of nanoparticles and the properties of polymers, composite materials create scaffolds that provide an optimal environment for cell proliferation, differentiation, and cell adhesion 19 . Various natural and synthetic fillers are used as strengthening agents to improve polymer scaffolds’ mechanical properties and bioactivity. Clay is considered one of the mineral fillers that will enhance the biological and mechanical properties of the scaffold significantly, and it has already been studied in bone cement processing 20 . Clay can be essential in bone regeneration and repair, increasing osteoblast differentiation and apatite deposition 20 . Also, the graphene oxide filler is highly compatible with the polymer matrix due to the attendance of polar groups on the surface of this material. The high exceptional area ratio and antibacterial properties make GO a promising material for developing bone tissue’s antimicrobial and antiviral surface 21 . GO is exfoliated from graphite and contains epoxide, carboxylic, and carbonyl ketone groups in its structural plane. Graphene and graphene-based materials have been investigated in various fields, such as bioimaging, biosensors, cancer treatment, drug delivery, and tissue engineering. Unique properties such as thermal conductivity, mechanical strength, high surface area, ease of operation, flexibility, biocompatibility, and antibacterial activity make it suitable for biological applications. GO has osteoconductive and osteoinductive ability to regulate osteoblast differentiation 22 , 23 . Also, this substance can force stem cells to undergo osteogenic differentiation even without adding a growth factor 24 . Hydroxyapatite contains basic elements such as calcium and phosphate, essential for bone growth. HAp particles increase cell proliferation and adhesion and improve bone tissue conduction 23 . Hydroxyapatite has unique properties, such as excellent biocompatibility and low toxicity 25 . Combining ceramic filler and polymer matrix in one material produces composites with polymer plasticity and calcium phosphate bioactivity 26 . Scaffolds containing HAp have stronger mechanical properties and higher cell proliferation than scaffolds containing TCP, which gives them a better function in bone regeneration in the early stages 27 . Nanoparticle content can be used to modify the properties of polymer nanocomposites 28 . Various magnetic nanoparticles have been developed recently for use in biological applications. Among these nanoparticles, iron oxide nanoparticles (Fe 3 O 4 ) have biocompatibility and good performance for medical applications. Many researchers have shown that the magnetic properties of Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles make it an ideal material for cell isolation and drug delivery, which also increases the effect of bone induction. Several studies have shown that integrating Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles with polymer fibers solves the compaction problem and improves their stability in biological applications 29 . The research fabricates nanocomposite scaffolds using CMC, Alg, PVA, magnetic clay nanoparticles modified with graphene oxide (CGF), and natural hydroxyapatite (HAp) to enhance mechanical properties. Characterization involved XRD, FT-IR, TGA, and SEM analyses, assessing porosity, swelling, degradation, and biomineralization, and the MTT test evaluated cell viability. In bone tissue research, the composites studied mainly combine a natural and synthetic polymer and an additive, each of which can only be used to improve one property. In this study, two biopolymers are compared in terms of the composition of scaffolds. Two additives are also used: HAp can be used to improve biomineralization, and CGF is a combination of several materials that can give many favorable properties to the polymer composite for use as a polymer scaffold. Having the required mechanical properties is one of the most important properties that bone tissue replacement scaffolds must possess. In addition to having the required mechanical properties, the scaffolds should possess other characteristics required by bone tissue, such as porosity, biocompatibility, swelling, and non-toxicity. A significant aspect of the research is that in addition to increasing compressive strength, attention has been paid to maintaining the required porosity of the bone tissue. A combination of graphene oxide, clay, hydroxyapatite, and the magnetic material Fe 3 O 4  has been used in this research as a new material. As a result of the combination of graphene oxide with clay, the compressive strength of the scaffold was increased significantly, and the presence of HAp led to an increase in biomineralization of the scaffold, whereas Fe 3 O 4  had a positive effect on the viability of the cells. The properties of natural bone differ significantly based on its type. For example, the compressive strength of cancellous bone ranges from 2 to 20 MPa. As a result, it is important to consider the scaffold’s mechanical properties when designing scaffolds for bone reconstruction and fracture healing to ensure a successful outcome 30 . It has been found that the scaffold that was prepared in this study has compressive strength within the acceptable range for cancellous bone. It is important to emphasize that the porosity of a hydrogel is a significant factor that profoundly affects the fate of bone cells 31 . A series of interconnected pores in this hydrogel facilitate the migration of cells, the permeability of nutrients, the removal of waste materials, and the penetration of blood vessels and nerves 32 . The porosity of cancellous bone ranges from 30 to 70% 33 . The porosity of the scaffolds prepared for this study is within an appropriate range for cancellous bone scaffolds. The prepared scaffolds had appropriate degradation rates because replacing the manufactured tissue with the original tissue would have to occur within a proper biodegradation range. Also, they exhibited a high degree of cell viability.

Materials and methods Materials Sodium alginate (Na-Alg) polymer with a molecular weight of 12,000–40,000 Da from Sigma Aldrich, sodium carboxymethyl cellulose with a molecular weight of 70,000 g/mol and a deacetylation degree of 90% from Samchun, Korea, and polyvinyl alcohol with a molecular weight of 72,000 g/mol and a hydrolysis degree of 95% from Merck were used in this study. Potassium hydrogen phosphate (KH 2 PO 4 ), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), and 25% glutaraldehyde as cross-linkers were obtained from Merck. The acids used in this study, including hydrochloric acid (HCl) 37%, phosphoric acid (H 3 PO 4 ), acetic acid (CH 3 COOH) 100%, and sulfuric acid (H 2 SO 4 ) 98%, were all obtained from Merck. Cloisite 30B clay was organically modified to be the clay used in the study, obtained from Southern Clay Company. K 2 HPO 4 .3H 2 O, KCl, NaCl, Na 2 SO 4 , MgCl 2 .6H 2 O, NaHCO 3 , CaCl 2 .2H 2 O, and TRIS salts prepared simulated body fluid purchased from Merck. Iron (II) chloride, Iron (III) chloride, KMnO 4 , and H 2 O 2  were used to modify clay. Synthesis of graphene oxide To prepare graphene oxide using the modified Hummer method. First, 3 g of graphite powder was added to the H 2 SO 4 /H 3 PO 4  mixture (40:360 ml), and then 18 g of KMnO 4  was added and stirred at 50 °C for 14 h with 100 rpm by a magnetic stirrer. The temperature of the resulting mixture was reduced to 25 °C, and then 30% H 2 O 2  was added to it, making the mixture light brown and stirred for 20 min. A centrifuge was used to separate the mixture, and several washes were done with distilled water to reach a neutral pH. In a vacuum oven, 24 h of drying time was spent at 70 °C on the synthesized graphene oxide 34 . Graphene oxide was synthesized in black and in powder form. Preparation of magnetically modified clay To prepare magnetically modified clay, 1 g of synthesized graphene oxide (GO) was sonicated in 100 mL of water for 35 min, while 2 g of Cloisite 30B clay was sonicated in 100 mL of water for 20 min. A solution of iron(III) chloride and iron(II) chloride in a 1:2 molar ratio was then added to the GO-clay mixture, which was heated and stirred at 90 °C for 20 min. The resulting mixture was gradually combined with a 4 M sodium hydroxide solution and stirred for 50 min. Finally, the modified clay was separated from the solution, washed several times with distilled water, and baked at 105 °C for 4 h. Preparation of natural hydroxyapatite (HAp) powder The chicken femur was cleaned entirely of meat and remaining materials to prepare natural hydroxyapatite powder and then boiled for 45 min in boiling water. The cleaned bones were placed in a furnace for 3 h at 900 °C with a nitrogen atmosphere. After the set time, the bones are removed from the oven, cooled to room temperature, and then powdered once they are at room temperature. The production efficiency of natural hydroxyapatite powder is 58%, and the product is white 35 . Preparation of nanocomposite scaffold The nanocomposite scaffolds were prepared using natural polymer materials, PVA, hydroxyapatite extracted from natural bone, and modified clay using a simple mixing method. For the preparation of the scaffold, a solution of 2 wt. in the first step. % of a natural polymer in distilled water was prepared by dissolving the polymer in the water and stirring the solution until the polymer was fully dissolved. It was also possible to prepare a solution of polyvinyl alcohol by dissolving PVA in distilled water at a temperature of 70 °C to prepare a solution of 0.2 wt. % of polyvinyl alcohol. HAp was dispersed in 1 v/v % acetic acid for 24 h to obtain a solution of 2 wt. % of hydroxyapatite. As soon as the materials had been prepared, they were added in the correct proportions, and the mixer was turned on for 12 h. 0.5 v/v % of glutaraldehyde was added to the prepped solution in 5% of the total solution volume to cross-link the polymer solution. The solution needs to be mixed for 20 min. A suspension was obtained by dispersing the modified clay in distilled water. In this method, the last step of adding CGF is included. The particles were evenly dispersed in the solution after adding CGF and then subjected to ultrasonic treatment for two hours. The solution was transferred into a falcon tube and dried using a freeze-dryer. After drying, the samples were washed with a 2 wt. % NaBH 4  solution and a 2 wt. % NaOH solution to neutralize any unreacted glutaraldehyde and excess acetic acid. The samples were then dried once more. The schematic representation of the PVA/Alg/Hap/CGF nanocomposite formation has been shown in the Scheme  1 . Scheme 1 Schematic representation of the formation of PVA/Alg/Hap/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds. Characterization of scaffolds Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) study To examine the functional and chemical properties of HAp, Fe 3 O 4 , GO, clay, Cloisite 30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 , Alg, CMC, PVA, and their interactions, we performed FTIR analysis on the prepared nanocomposite scaffolds. Infrared spectra of the samples, mixed with KBr powder, were recorded using a Bruker Tensor 27 spectrophotometer (Germany). This FTIR instrument offers a measurement resolution of 0.1 cm⁻ 1 , and its readings are based on absorbance. X-ray diffraction (XRD) study XRD analysis evaluated the crystallinity of PVA, CMC, Alg, HAp, clay, and C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  samples and the prepared nanocomposite scaffolds. The analysis was performed using a Krisallofex D500 diffractometer (Siemens, Germany), which employed Cu-Kα radiation at 25 mA, operating at 40 kV, and scanned over a range of 4–80°. The device had a scan step of 0.05° and a measurement accuracy of 0.04 deg/sec. Morphology study of scaffold samples by SEM analysis The scaffolds were prepared by freeze-drying and then coated with a thin layer of gold after being cut into thin sheets. Once the samples were positioned in the microscope, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (MIRA3, TESCAN, Brno, Czech Republic) were used to examine the morphology of the scaffolds. Furthermore, the presence and distribution of phosphorus and calcium elements within the nanocomposite scaffold were analyzed using energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping. A voltage of 30 kV was applied to the SEM device. Also, to image the amount of cell culture by SEM, uniform pieces of the scaffolds were cut and immersed in 70% ethanol solution for 30 min for sterilization. In the next step, the samples were washed three times with PBS to remove excess ethanol and then transferred to a 6-well cell culture plate. A cell plate with a density of 50,000 cells per cell plate was then used to culture the MSCs on scaffolds. As a final step, the samples were cultured in an FBS medium with 1% penicillin–streptomycin and 10% DMEM in an incubator for 72 h. They were incubated for 72 h at 37 °C. After this time, the supernatant of all the wells of the 6-well plate was removed, and after washing with PBS, they were fixed with 2.5% glutaraldehyde for 20 min. After removing the fixative solution, they were dried under the hood and in the environment. Finally, the morphological images of the samples prepared according to this section were examined by SEM. Mechanical properties study The mechanical properties of the scaffold samples were evaluated by measuring the compressive strength prepared by freeze-drying. To assess the material’s compressive strength, cylinder samples measuring 12 mm in height and 6 mm in diameter were cut. During a compression test, a force with 0.5 mm per minute speed and power of 25 kN was used to test the material’s compressive strength at 25 °C. A universal machine model was used to carry out mechanical tests, Al-7000-Taiwand. Three samples of each scaffold were prepared to perform the compressive strength test, and all three samples were tested. Their average compressive strength was reported as the compressive strength of the polymer scaffold. The error value was 0.1 MPa. Measuring the porosity of scaffolds Using the liquid displacement method, we could determine the porosity of scaffolds 36 . Scaffolds were cut into one cubic cm square piece. A displacement liquid was employed in this case, and it was ethanol because it does not dissolve the scaffolds while it is being used. To determine the porosity of the cut scaffolds, the scaffolds were placed in a graduated container containing ethanol with volume (V 1 ) to measure the porosity. Using the total volume of ethanol and scaffold (V 2 ), we calculated the volume difference V 2 -V 1 . After exiting the scaffold from the cylinder containing ethanol, the remaining volume of the ethanol in the cylinder was measured by using the following formula: V 3 . According to the equation below, the scaffold porosity could be estimated using the following equation: 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
