Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) study To examine the functional and chemical properties of HAp, Fe 3 O 4 , GO, clay, Cloisite 30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 , Alg, CMC, PVA, and their interactions, we performed FTIR analysis on the prepared nanocomposite scaffolds. Infrared spectra of the samples, mixed with KBr powder, were recorded using a Bruker Tensor 27 spectrophotometer (Germany). This FTIR instrument offers a measurement resolution of 0.1 cm⁻ 1 , and its readings are based on absorbance.

X-ray diffraction (XRD) study XRD analysis evaluated the crystallinity of PVA, CMC, Alg, HAp, clay, and C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  samples and the prepared nanocomposite scaffolds. The analysis was performed using a Krisallofex D500 diffractometer (Siemens, Germany), which employed Cu-Kα radiation at 25 mA, operating at 40 kV, and scanned over a range of 4–80°. The device had a scan step of 0.05° and a measurement accuracy of 0.04 deg/sec.

Morphology study of scaffold samples by SEM analysis The scaffolds were prepared by freeze-drying and then coated with a thin layer of gold after being cut into thin sheets. Once the samples were positioned in the microscope, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (MIRA3, TESCAN, Brno, Czech Republic) were used to examine the morphology of the scaffolds. Furthermore, the presence and distribution of phosphorus and calcium elements within the nanocomposite scaffold were analyzed using energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping. A voltage of 30 kV was applied to the SEM device. Also, to image the amount of cell culture by SEM, uniform pieces of the scaffolds were cut and immersed in 70% ethanol solution for 30 min for sterilization. In the next step, the samples were washed three times with PBS to remove excess ethanol and then transferred to a 6-well cell culture plate. A cell plate with a density of 50,000 cells per cell plate was then used to culture the MSCs on scaffolds. As a final step, the samples were cultured in an FBS medium with 1% penicillin–streptomycin and 10% DMEM in an incubator for 72 h. They were incubated for 72 h at 37 °C. After this time, the supernatant of all the wells of the 6-well plate was removed, and after washing with PBS, they were fixed with 2.5% glutaraldehyde for 20 min. After removing the fixative solution, they were dried under the hood and in the environment. Finally, the morphological images of the samples prepared according to this section were examined by SEM.

Mechanical properties study The mechanical properties of the scaffold samples were evaluated by measuring the compressive strength prepared by freeze-drying. To assess the material’s compressive strength, cylinder samples measuring 12 mm in height and 6 mm in diameter were cut. During a compression test, a force with 0.5 mm per minute speed and power of 25 kN was used to test the material’s compressive strength at 25 °C. A universal machine model was used to carry out mechanical tests, Al-7000-Taiwand. Three samples of each scaffold were prepared to perform the compressive strength test, and all three samples were tested. Their average compressive strength was reported as the compressive strength of the polymer scaffold. The error value was 0.1 MPa.

