Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) study The thermal stability of the polymer raw materials and nanocomposite scaffolds was assessed using TGA. A thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA-PL, TGA 1500, Canada) was employed for this analysis, conducted in a nitrogen atmosphere within the 25–600°C temperature range.

Investigation of scaffold biomineralization using simulated body fluid (SBF) The performance of the scaffold bone graft was assessed using simulated body fluid (SBF), which was prepared following established protocols with chemical reagents. SBF is essential for examining the formation of biomaterials on polymer scaffolds. To prepare the solution, 1000 mL of SBF was made by adding 700 mL of deionized water into a plastic container in a water bath. It is recommended to cover the container with either a glass or plastic lid while stirring; for the process to be successful, the solution should be kept at a consistent temperature of 36.5 °C throughout. Adding the ingredients in order is essential; once each ingredient is dissolved completely, the next one should be added, and so on. As soon as the ingredients were added, hydrochloric acid was used in the amount of 0–5 ml with an additive solution to achieve a pH of 7.4 in the resulting solution after adding the ingredients. A total of 1000 mL of solution was prepared by adding water to a flask and shaking it at 20 °C to reach the desired volume. The solution was then transferred to a polyethylene container and stored in a refrigerator at 5–10 °C. Scaffold samples were immersed in the SBF solution and incubated at 37 °C for 14 days. After incubation, the samples were removed, rinsed with distilled water, and dried using the freeze-drying method. Finally, EDX and SEM techniques were employed to evaluate the biomineralization capability of the scaffold samples 38 .

Assessment of toxicity and cell viability First, uniform pieces of scaffolds were cut and immersed in 70% ethanol solution for 30 min to sterilize them. Eventually, we must continue using PBS to remove any remaining ethanol from the system and transfer it to a 96-well cell culture plate. Then, mesenchymal stem cells were cultured on the scaffolds with a cell density of 1 × 10 4  cells per cell plate. After collecting the samples, they were cultured inside the incubator in a DMEM medium containing 1% penicillin–streptomycin and 10% FBS. They were incubated for 72 h at 37 °C. After 72 h, the supernatant of all the wells of the 96-well plate was removed, and after washing with PBS to each well, 0.2 ml of MTT solution (5 mg/mm) was added, and the wells were kept in the incubator for four hours. It was incubated at 37 °C. A solution of 0.1 mL DMSO was then substituted for the MTT solution for 30 min. Finally, the absorbance was measured by an ELISA reader at a wavelength of 570 nm to determine cell viability.

Results and discussion Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis In the structure of HAp prepared from the chicken thigh in Fig.  1 a, the peak in the wave number range of 1090–1045 cm -1  indicates the vibrations of PO 4 3-  in the structure of HAp, and the vibration of -OH is observed in 3570 cm -1 39 , 40 . The 568 cm -1  and 600–640 cm -1  peaks correspond to the bending and stretching vibrations of PO 4 3-  ions 41 . Meanwhile, the vibrations at 1400 cm -1  indicate the presence of carbonate, which influences the bioactive characteristics of HAp. HAp forms peaks that contain cations of Ca 2+ , which are associated with HAp 42 . Figure  1 a shows a spectrum of graphene oxide with peaks at 2920 and 2800 cm -1 , which correspond to the peaks of the -CH 2  group in graphene oxide. 3400 cm -1  peak is related with the OH group, 1574 cm -1  peak with C=C bond, and 1711 cm -1  peak with C=O bond 43 , 44 . The 1160 and 1050 cm -1  peaks in the diagram above correspond to the vibrations of C-OH and C-O, respectively 45 , 46 . The FT-IR spectrum of Fe 3 O 4  material, shown in Fig.  1 a, features characteristic bands at 3430 cm -1  and 1611 cm -1  associated with hydroxyl groups. The 590 cm -1  peak represents Fe-O vibrations, while the 3421 and 1569 cm -1  peaks are also attributed to hydroxyl groups in the Fe 3 O 4  compound 47 , 48 . The 2920 and 2850 cm -1  peaks are also linked to C-H stretching 49 . The FT-IR spectrum of the clay is shown in Fig.  1 a, where the peak at 1040 cm -1  is assigned to Si vibrations in the Si-O band. In the 3634–3393 cm -1  region of the FTIR spectrum, there is a broad absorption peak assigned to the OH stretching vibration. Two peaks at 2920 and 2854 cm -1  have been assigned to compounds that contain linear aliphatic chains and are indicative of clay’s organic modification 18 . Absorption peaks at 525, 460, and 1470 cm -1  were identified, corresponding to the vibrations of Si-O-Al, Si-O-Mg, and carbonate (CaCO 3  or Mg(CO 3 ) 2 ), respectively 50 . In the FT-IR spectrum of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  (CGF) material, a peak at 12,920 cm -1  was observed, attributed to C-H vibrations in graphene oxide 51 . Additionally, the absorption peak at 1460 cm -1  is linked to the C-O bond in graphene oxide 52 , while the peak at 1040 cm -1  corresponds to silicon in Cloisite 30B-modified clay 18 . After placing Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles and graphene oxide in the clay structure, the intensity and range of the absorption peaks in the clay structure changed significantly, which can be caused by the interaction between the functional groups of the clay surface, the iron, and graphene oxide nanoparticles. In addition, absorption vibrations in the said magnetic structure composites originating from Fe-O vibrations have been seen, confirming the presence of Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles in the structure 52 . Fig. 1 FT-IR spectra ( a ) HAp, CGF, Cloisite 30B, Fe 3 O 4 , GO, ( b ) CMC, Alg and, PVA biopolymers, ( c ) PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp and PVA/Alg scaffolds, and ( d ) PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF, PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC nanocomposite scaffolds. The FT-IR spectrum of the alginate biopolymer, shown in Fig.  1 b, includes peaks at 2920, 883, and 12,109 cm -1 , corresponding to the -OH group 53 . Peaks at 1418 and 1627 cm -1  are associated with the symmetric stretching of the carboxyl group and the carboxylate group (C=O) 54 , while the peak at 1125 cm -1  relates to C-O and C-C bonds 55 . The FT-IR spectrum of carboxymethyl cellulose, also depicted in Fig.  1 b, displays a strong, broad absorption band at 13,439 cm -1  due to -OH group stretching. The peak at 2919 cm -1  corresponds to C-H stretching vibrations 56 , 57 , the strong band at 1609 cm -1  indicates the presence of the -COO -  group, and absorption bands at 11,423 and 1328 cm -1  are attributed to -CH 2  and -OH vibrations. The peak at 11,059 cm -1  is also linked to CH-O-CH 2  bond stretching 57 . The FT-IR spectrum of the scaffold, composed of polyvinyl alcohol and sodium alginate, is shown in Fig.  1 c, highlighting the scaffold’s structural features. In this spectrum, the peak at 3423 cm -1  corresponds to OH present in sodium alginate and PVA, and the peak at 1628 cm -1  is assigned to the presence of -COO -  in alginate. The 850 and 1108 cm -1  peaks can be related to C-C and C-O of alginate and PVA 53 , 58 , 59 . The 1431 and 2923 cm -1  peaks represent CH-OH and CH in PVA or OH of alginate, respectively 60 , 61 . All the peaks of both PVA and alginate biopolymers were also present in the scaffold of the combination of these two biopolymers, and they show a slight shift, which indicates the mixing of materials and the formation of a polymer blend. In the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold shown in Fig.  1 c, the new peak appearing at 1569 cm -1  is assigned to the presence of the -PO 4 3-  group. The peak in 1043 cm -1  also indicates the attendance of -PO 4 3-  in the scaffold. The peak at 609 cm -1  corresponds to the -OH group associated with hydroxyapatite, while the peak at 1425 cm -1  represents -CO 3 2 ⁻ related to HAp 35 . The presence of these new peaks in the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold, compared to the PVA/Alg scaffold, confirms the incorporation of HAp. In the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold, additional peaks have emerged alongside those from the previous scaffold. Peaks at 431 and 471 cm -1  are attributed to Si-O-Mg and Si-O-Al vibrations 50 , indicating the formation of Cloisite 30B clay bonds within the new scaffold structure. Vibrations around 571 cm -1  correspond to the Fe-O bond, while peaks at 1429 and 2935 cm -1  are associated with C-O and C-H vibrations in graphene oxide (GO), confirming the presence of polymers 52 . These observations verify the incorporation of CGF into the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Additionally, slight shifts in the PVA, Alg, and HAp peaks suggest effective integration of the materials within the scaffold. Since no new peaks appeared in the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold compared to the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold, it can be inferred that CGF was incorporated into the polymer matrix through physical interactions. Figure  1 d presents the FT-IR spectra of PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF, PVA/CMC/HAp, and PVA/CMC scaffolds. In the PVA/CMC scaffold spectrum, the broad peak at 3420 cm -1  corresponds to the -OH stretching vibrations of the hydroxyl groups in PVA and CMC. In contrast, the peak around 1330 cm -1  is associated with C-O vibrations of the acetyl groups in PVA 56 , 57 , 62 . The peak in the 1603 cm-1 region is associated with the symmetrical bending of the C-O bond in PVA and CMC 62 , 63 , and the C-O absorption band in the 1096 cm -1  region is due to the attendance of CMC and PVA. The 846, 1426, and 2938 cm -1  peaks show C-C, CH-OH, and CH in PVA and CMC, respectively 64 , 65 . The displacement of the peaks compared to the peak of the original samples shows the combination of materials and the formation of a polymer blend. It is expected that hydrogen bonding will occur between PVA and CMC, and these forces occur in the bending region of OH and -COO- functional group 66 . Comparing the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold with that of the PVA/CMC scaffold reveals that the peak at 1603 cm -1  becomes sharper, and a new peak appears around 570 cm -1 , corresponding to the bending and stretching vibrations of the -PO 4 3 ⁻ group 41 . These peaks confirm the presence of HAp in the scaffold. Furthermore, the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold shows the emergence of a new peak at 496 cm -1 , attributed to the vibrations of Si bonds in Cloisite 30B clay, indicating the incorporation of CGF into the final PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold 50 . The mixing of CGF into the polymer matrix of this nanocomposite scaffold was achieved through physical interactions. X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis XRD analysis was done to identify the crystal structure of the samples, including the different phases of the crystal regions. In Fig.  2 a, the XRD spectrum shows the structure of natural HAp, in which peaks with different intensities appear and shows that the natural HAp powder has a crystalline structure. The crystalline phases “102” and “002” of hydroxyapatite were identified at 2θ angles of 26.28 and 26.02°, respectively 42 . Also, other peaks of crystalline phases of this material include “211”, “112”, “300”, “202”, “310”, “222”, “312”, “213”, “321”, “410”, “402”, and “004” respectively in the values of the 2θ angles 31.9, 23.3, 33.06, 34.26, 39.9, 46.8, 48.2, 49.6, 50.6, and 51.4. 52.2 and 53.3° are visible 41 . The index peak of GO material is created at the 2θ angle of 12.3° 44 , 67 , which indicates the “001” crystal phase 68 . In the XRD spectrum of Fe 3 O 4 , the peak at a 2θ angle of 30° corresponds to the crystal phase “220”, while peaks at 2θ angles of 37.7, 54.6, 57.16, 74.2, 63.3, and 42.8° represent the crystal phases “311”, “422”, “511”, “533”, “440”, and '“400”, respectively 56 . The XRD spectrum of Cloisite 30B clay, the characteristic peak at a 2θ angle of 5.02°, corresponds to the ‘001’ crystalline phase 69 , 70 . There are peaks in the structure of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  magnetic composite with different intensities, and these peaks match those seen in the structure of the components. This indicates that the components are correctly interrelated in the composite structure. In the composite structure, the peaks observed at 2θ angles of 63.1, 53.8, and 35.7° correspond to Fe 3 O 4  particles, while the peak at 11.84° is associated with graphene oxide. The peak at the 2θ angle of 6° is related to the attendance of Cloisite 30B clay in the composite. In the XRD spectrum of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  material, the peak of the GO index is significantly weakened, which is caused by the separation of the GO layers and the placement of Fe 3 O 4  particles between its layers, which indicates the successful preparation of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  composite 52 . Also, the peak indicating Cloisite 30B clay has weakened, suggesting that its layers are separated. The XRD spectrum of alginate can be seen in Fig.  2 b. Alginate index peaks have appeared in the 2θ range of 19.08, 32.2, 33.9, 36, and 48.84° 71 , 72 . The X-ray diffraction of CMC biopolymer is shown in Fig.  2 b, and the main diffraction of this material is observed at 2θ of 20.04° 63 . As can be seen in this figure, alginate polymer has sharper peaks than CMC polymer. Figure  2 c shows the XRD spectrum of PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg scaffolds. In the spectrum of the PVA/Alg scaffold, the index peak appeared in this scaffold in the range of 2θ of 19.8°; both biopolymers from PVA and Alg were found to be present in the scaffold structure. Also, the peaks at 2θ of 22.56, 31.8, 33.86, and 46.2° are due to alginate biopolymer 63 , 71 . By comparing the peak of the PVA/Alg scaffold with the peaks of PVA and Alg, it can be seen that the peaks of the polymer blend scaffold have been slightly shifted and reduced in intensity compared to the original samples, which shows that the biopolymers of this scaffold have interacted well and a suitable polymer blend has been created. XRD spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold is shown in Fig.  2 b. By comparing this spectrum with the spectrum of the PVA/Alg scaffold, it can be seen that the peak at the 2θ angle of 31.8° has become much sharper, which shows the effective presence of HAp in the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold. Also, a new peak has been created at the 2θ angle of 49.5°, which was absent in the PVA/Alg scaffold 73 . In the XRD spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, a new peak at a 2θ angle of 5° is observed, indicating the presence of Cloisite 30B clay 69 . Additionally, the peak at 11.4° corresponds to graphene oxide (GO) 44 , while the peaks at 42.02, 57.9, 63.4, and 74.8° are associated with the Fe 3 O 4  crystalline structure 74 . These peaks confirm the inclusion of CGF material components in the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. However, the slight weakening of the Cloisite 30B clay peak suggests the formation of an exfoliated structure within the nanocomposite scaffold. The XRD spectra of the PVA and CMC scaffolds are shown in Fig.  2 d. The peak of the PVA/CMC scaffold appears at 19.78°, which confirms the presence of both PVA and CMC polymers, but the peak that appears is broader than the PVA and CMC peaks and also shifts concerning both materials, which indicates a strong interaction between PVA and CMC. In the XRD spectrum of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold, the peaks that appear at 08.32, 8.33, 9.46, 56.40, and 5.49° are related to the presence and effect of hydroxyapatite in the scaffold 39 . By comparing the XRD spectra of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold with the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold, it is observed that the small peak that appears at 5° is affected by the presence of C30B, and the peak at 8.11° is related to GO. These newly formed peaks confirm the presence of CGF in the scaffold, but compared to CGF, the peak corresponding to Cloisite 30B has almost disappeared, indicating the separation of the clay layers and the formation of the exfoliated structure of the nanocomposite. Fig. 2 XRD diagrams of ( a ) HAp, CGF, C30B, Fe 3 O 4 , and GO, ( b ) CMC, ALg, and PVA biopolymers, ( c ) PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp and PVA/Alg scaffolds, and d) PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF, PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC scaffolds. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) analysis The morphological properties of HAp obtained from the femur of chicken, modified clay, and synthesized scaffolds have been evaluated using FESEM analysis and EDX analysis. As seen in Fig.  3 a, the produced HAp has a crystalline structure. In Fig.  3 b, as seen in the EDX analysis of HAp, the elements Mg, Na, and Zn are observed in addition to the main elements P and Ca. Magnesium, sodium, and zinc elements in the structure of hydroxyapatite can play a very important role in influencing its biological function. Magnesium impurities in HAp can promote osteoblast growth and multiplication in bone tissue. Zinc incorporation into the HAp structure is highly beneficial for bone regeneration, as it promotes the proliferation of osteoclasts and osteoblasts while enhancing bone formation. The attendance of Na improves the biocompatibility and cellular characteristics of HAp 41 . As it is clear from the SEM images and EDX analysis of clay modified with Fe 3 O 4  and GO in Fig.  3 c–d, clay and graphene oxide have a layered structure, and after adding and changing it with compounds containing Fe (II) and Fe (III), Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles with different sizes and spherical morphology have been created on the clay surface. Therefore, Cloisite 30B particles, graphene oxide, and Fe 3 O 4  have adequate and proper interaction, and the peaks belonging to O, Fe, Si, and C elements show the modification of clay with graphene oxide and Fe 3 O 4 . Fig. 3 ( a ) SEM image of hydroxyapatite prepared from the chicken femur, ( b ) EDX analysis results of natural hydroxyapatite, ( c ) SEM image of C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 , and ( d ) EDX analysis result of C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 . Alginate biopolymer is suitable for hard tissue engineering, has no toxic properties, and is biocompatible 75 . Figure  4 a shows the scaffold morphology produced from the PVA/Alg polymer blend. This scaffold has a proper porosity distribution, and large and small porosity can be seen in the image. Figure  4 b illustrates the morphology of the PVA/Alg/HAp polymer scaffold, showing that adding HAp slightly reduces its porosity. The SEM image of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold in Fig.  4 c reveals a further reduction in porosity and a denser structure attributed to the inclusion of GO nanoparticles and Cloisite 30B clay. Additionally, CMC, a biopolymer with high water absorption and biodegradable bonds, plays a role in adjusting the scaffold’s porous structure and enhancing its swelling capacity 76 . Figure  4 d displays the morphology of the PVA/CMC blend scaffold. As is apparent in the image, this scaffold has a perfect distribution of porosity, which is very suitable for the stage of cell culture and bone tissue regeneration. Large and small pores are seen simultaneously in this scaffold. CMC biopolymer in a hydrogel blend with PVA has increased the pore size of scaffold pores. The presence of CMC in the hydrogel absorbs water, resulting in the formation of more ice crystals in the scaffolds, and after sublimation, larger voids are formed in the scaffold 76 . Figure  4 e also shows the morphology of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold, whose porosity has been reduced due to the addition of hydroxyapatite. Finally, Fig.  4 f shows the scaffold morphology from adding CGF nanoparticles to the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold. The increase of these nanoparticles causes a slight reduction in the porosity of the nanocomposite scaffold, and a suitable distribution of the size of the pores can be seen. By comparing the prepared PVA/Alg and PVA/CMC scaffolds, it can be seen that the porosity distribution in the PVA/CMC scaffold is higher, which shows the suitability of this scaffold for cell growth and proliferation. Porosity is one of the important factors in determining mechanical properties. Reducing the porosity significantly enhanced the scaffold’s mechanical properties 77 , 78 . Therefore, the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds have higher mechanical properties than those without CGF nanoparticles. As stated in the XRD analysis, the alginate polymer had sharper peaks than the CMC polymer, and the composites containing the alginate biopolymer also showed sharper peaks than the scaffolds containing the CMC biopolymer, which caused more pores in the scaffolds containing alginate, which can also be seen in the SEM images in Fig.  4 . Fig. 4 SEM images of various scaffolds: ( a ) PVA/Alg, ( b ) PVA/Alg/HAp, ( c ) PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, ( d ) PVA/CMC, ( e ) PVA/CMC/HAp, and ( f ) PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF. One of the essential parameters of an ideal scaffold is its porosity so that nutrients and oxygen can easily pass through its pores and reach the cells, and waste products can pass through them and be excreted. The size of the pores should not be so large that they impair mechanical strength nor so small that they become blocked by cells and prevent the penetration of cells and vessels into the interior of the scaffold. Figure  5 a–c shows the microstructure and dot-mapping of PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffolds. Figure  5 a shows the scaffold morphology with large pores; this scaffold comprises only two polymers: PVA and Alg. Figure  5 b presents the morphology of the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold. As it is clear by comparing this scaffold with the scaffold of the previous form, the scaffold’s porosity is reduced, and the accumulation of white material indicates hydroxyapatite particles on the surface of this polymer scaffold. Also, this figure shows the distribution of the elements from the EDX analysis in the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold. Ca (green dots) and P (red dots) elements are seen due to the attendance of HAp in the scaffold, which shows a uniform distribution of these elements in the scaffolding. Figure  5 c shows the microstructure of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold, which is still porous. However, it has relatively low porosity, and HAp and CGF nanoparticles are visible. In this figure, the distribution of various elements in the microstructure of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold can be seen, where Fe and Si elements indicate the attendance of CGF nanomaterial in the scaffold, and P and Ca elements indicate the HAp material, which has a uniform distribution. Fig. 5 ( a – c ) SEM with dot-mapping images of the PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffolds. Figure  6  shows the SEM microstructure and dot-mapping of scaffolds containing PVA and CMC biopolymers. Figure  6 a shows the structure and the distribution of elements of the PVA/CMC scaffold, and Fig.  6 b shows distribution of elements of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold. As it is clear from these figures, this scaffold appeared to have less porosity than the previous scaffold due to the presence of HAp. Also, in the distribution of elements in this scaffold presented in these figures, the presence of Ca and P elements was proof of the presence of HAp in this scaffold. The uniform distribution of calcium and phosphorus elements is also observed, showing the homogenous HAp dispersion in the scaffold. The last SEM image is the microstructure of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold. The uniform distribution of P, Ca, Fe, and Si elements in this scaffold in Fig.  6 c indicates the effective presence of CGF and HAp nanoparticles and their homogeneous distribution within the polymer matrix of this scaffold. As seen in Fig.  6 b and c, there is no accumulation of nanomaterials in these scaffolds, which indicates a very homogeneous distribution of HAp particles and CGF nanoparticles in the scaffolds, which can cause higher mechanical properties due to the reduction of stress concentration points. Also, in this scaffold, due to the homogeneous distribution of HAp particles in its polymer matrix, we will witness more and more homogeneous biomineralization. Fig. 6 ( a – c ) SEM microstructures and dot-mapping results of PVA/CMC, PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffolds. Compariosn of the compressive strength, porosity, and swelling of scaffold samples A scaffold ideal for bone tissue engineering must have appropriate mechanical properties to support tissue growth when subjected to mechanical loading 79 . GO-containing scaffolds have excellent thermal and mechanical properties because GO is well dispersed in the polymer scaffold 80 , 81 . The wrinkled surface of GO sheets leads to high entanglement and adhesion between the polymer matrix and GO 82 . The presence of GO in nanocomposites significantly improves their tensile strength, Young’s modulus, and compressive strength. Improving the nanocomposite’s mechanical properties can be attributed to the interaction between the carboxyl groups in GO and the groups in polysaccharides 83 . Therefore, the weak mechanical properties of the polymer matrix can be increased by adding GO 84 . Hydrogen bonding between the hydroxyl and carbonyl groups on the GO sheets and the oxygen-containing functional groups of the polymer chains is possible, increasing the scaffold’s mechanical strength. The presence of GO/Fe 3 O 4  in the scaffold can improve the strain and stress of the scaffold 52 . The presence of graphene oxide in the nanocomposite enables it to absorb more energy before failure, enhance toughness, and lead to improved structural integrity 85 . Increased pore wall thickness and reduced porosity improve the mechanical properties of scaffolds, as Clay reduces porosity 86 . Clay’s presence causes the polymer scaffold’s cell pores to collapse. After the complete collapse of the cell pores, the walls of the opposite cell pores come into contact with each other and compress the solid more, creating a denser region, which ultimately addition the scaffold’s mechanical properties 87 . The results show that the incorporation of C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 , with the combined presence of graphene oxide, Clay, and Fe 3 O 4 , has enhanced the compressive strength of the scaffold. Figure  7 a–b shows the maximum compressive strength values of the scaffolds containing Alg and CMC biopolymers, and based on these results, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold with 12 MPa has the highest mechanical strength. The SEM images from this scaffold showed it had the least porosity. CGF and HAp nanoparticles were well distributed in this scaffold, which reduced the stress concentration points to the lowest value and increased the scaffold’s mechanical properties. The maximum compressive strength values reported for scaffold CS-Gel/nHA-PANi were 5.06 MPa 88 , for scaffold BC-g(Fe3O4/GO) were 11.6 MPa 52 , and for scaffold PLA/HA, about 12 MPa were obtained 89 . For the scaffold, CS-Gel/nHA/MMT, 5.11 MPa were obtained 86 . These cases indicate that the maximum compressive strength value obtained in this study is a suitable number compared to similar works and is also within the range of cancellous bone (2–20 MPa). Fig. 7 ( a – b ) Results of compressive strength, ( c – d ) porosity, and ( e – f ) percentage of water absorption in different prepared scaffolds. Porosity is an important factor when using polymer scaffolds to enhance cell culture. Porosity is helpful for blood vessels and cell growth. Still, if there are many macropores in the scaffold, it will cause a noticeable drop in its mechanical properties. Therefore, choosing a scaffold with a suitable ratio of large and small pores is essential for bone tissue regeneration 90 . For bone defects, polymer scaffolds with a porosity higher than 80% are unsuitable 91 , 92 . According to the results shown in Fig.  7 c–d, the porosity of the final PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffolds was 79 and 72%, respectively, which is to provide an opportunity for nutrient transport and cell migration is sufficient 84 . As stated in the previous section, the attendance of clay in the scaffold reduces its porosity 93 . It is possible to occupy the free -OH groups in the polymer matrix by adding GO, which increases the hydrophobicity of the nanocomposite scaffold and, as a result, reduces the percentage of porosity 94 . The control of the swelling behavior of the scaffolds, just like the control of the porosity parameter, is also of utmost importance if the scaffolds are to maintain their integrity in interaction with biological fluids. Thus, the swelling of the scaffolds can be attributed to the properties inherent in the constituent materials, which are responsible for causing the swelling. This allows control of swelling ability at the level of material selection 11 . The overall porosity and pore size are increased by swelling, resulting in the maximum possible surface area of the scaffold. When a sample shows a high degree of swelling, it can accept the maximum cell injection into the scaffold and create the maximum cell growth on the surface of the scaffold 95 . A proper interaction between the scaffold and the physiological fluid is essential for initial cell attachment, ultimately leading to better cell proliferation 91 . As shown in Fig.  7 e–f, the most swelling was obtained in CMC scaffolds. Considering that only the type of biopolymer used was different in the prepared polymer scaffolds, the results of Fig.  7 e–f show that the scaffold containing alginate biopolymer has the lowest amount of water absorption, and the scaffold containing CMC achieves higher swelling. CMC has exceptional water absorption properties, the bonds of which are biodegradable, and is derived from carbohydrate sources 76 ; therefore, due to its very hydrophilic nature, it increases the size of scaffold pores in a hydrogel-containing PVA, which, in turn, increases the amount of scaffold swelling. In PVA/CMC scaffolds, negatively charged carbonyl groups (CH 2 COO - ) from CMC and uncharged carboxyl groups on PVA are expected to contribute to the hydrophilicity of the scaffolds 76 . The incorporation of HAp reduces the hydrophilicity of the scaffolds through the binding of calcium and phosphate with the –COOH and –NH 2  groups of the polymers. Also, due to the addition of clay, the interaction between polymer macromolecules and water molecules is limited, reducing the water content absorbed by the scaffold. Scaffolds containing GO can absorb more water than scaffolds containing only polymer. High water absorption in scaffolds containing GO is due to many oxygen functional groups on GO; this process forms a hydrogen bond between the water and the GO. Incorporation of GO into polymer scaffolds is desirable to improve water absorption, which can help the absorption of drugs or nutrients into porous scaffolds 84 . The swelling values obtained in this study for scaffolds PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF are 2056 and 2412, respectively, compared to scaffold gelatin/chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol/nano-hydroxyapatite, where the swelling value was reported to be 700 90  which is a suitable value for bone substitute tissue. For scaffold chitosan/cellulose nanocrystals/halloysite nanotubes, the swelling value is reported to be 2200 91 . Biodegradation measurements, and Thermogravimetric (TGA) analysis The disintegration behavior of a scaffold is an essential factor in achieving a successful regeneration process, as it must match the rate of tissue formation. Scaffold samples must be stable enough to withstand regeneration 67 . Polyvinyl alcohol is a synthetic biodegradable polymer 96 . When composite scaffolds are soaked in PBS solution, they hydrolyze chemical bonds within them, causing weight loss due to the hydrolysis 90 . As a function of the time the scaffolds were submerged in PBS solution, the weight loss of the scaffolds was determined, as shown in Fig.  8 a–b. As shown in this figure, in the first week, all scaffolds had a higher degradation rate, but after that, their degradation rate decreased. PBS medium reduces the weight of the scaffolds rapidly since physical and hydrogen bonds mainly connect them. HAp synthesized from chicken femur is crystalline, as shown in the results of SEM analysis. This high crystallization of HAp can reduce its degradation kinetics, and therefore, HAp can help maintain the scaffold’s stability 97 . Adding HAp to the polymer scaffold containing polysaccharides reduces the buffer access to the sites of the polysaccharide molecules, which reduces the degradation of the scaffold 88 . Therefore, scaffolds made of different biopolymers containing HAp show low degradation 11 . The results for the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold show that adding HAp has reduced the degradation rate of the scaffold. The ions released from HAp can reduce the rate of the degradation process and thus increase the bioactivity of the polymer scaffold 98 . Adding clay to the nanocomposite scaffold improves the degradation rate of the scaffold, and increasing the hydrophilicity of the scaffolds containing clay increases the diffusion of PBS inside the scaffolds and causes hydrolytic degradation 20 . Adding clay modified with Fe 3 O 4  and GO reduces the degradation rate of the final scaffold. The clay plates form a strong interaction with the macromolecular chains of the biopolymer scaffold, which causes the hydrophilic groups to be consumed and the amount of solvent absorption to decrease, which protects the macromolecules from the hydrolysis process. Also, clay increases the stability of the polymer network 67 . Adding GO reduces the scaffold degradation rate 92 , 99 . The decrease in degradation of the polymer scaffold when adding GO is probably because GO sheets prevent the penetration of destructive substances in the phosphate buffer solution. In addition, the polymer matrix can have a more regular arrangement in the presence of GO, which is desirable for less penetration of destructive substances 99 . The increase of Fe 3 O 4 /GO reduces the degradation of the porous scaffold 52 . Alginate biopolymer has high biodegradability and is used as a drug and growth factor carrier 13 . Therefore, the PVA/Alg scaffold showed high biodegradability, which was reduced by adding HAp and CGF; however, for the final PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, the biodegradation rate is in the appropriate range. Because CMC biopolymer has high water absorption ability and suitable biodegradable links 76 , the highest degradation was obtained in the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Fig. 8 ( a – b ) Degradation amount of scaffolds containing Alg and CMC under laboratory conditions in PBS in periods of 7, 14, and 21 days, ( c – e ) TGA curves of polymer and nanocomposite scaffolds. Thermograms of PVA, CMC, and Alg polymer materials are given in Fig.  8 c. The alginate thermogram shows that alginate decomposition started at around 25 °C, followed by weight loss due to the degrading of alginate chains and its monomers at 203 and 552 °C, respectively 100 , 101 . In the 203 to 256 °C temperature range, the weight loss is due to removing the -COO groups. The residue consists of elements that decompose above 900 °C 100 . In the TGA diagram related to polyvinyl alcohol in Fig.  8 c, the weight loss below 100 °C is related to removing physically absorbed water. Due to this polymer’s ease of absorption, a relatively significant amount of water has been absorbed. The main weight loss occurs in the second stage within the 211 to 331 °C temperature range, associated with the breakdown of PVA polymer side chains. The third stage of weight loss occurs between 400 and 490 °C, corresponding to the degradation of the polymer’s main chains 102 . Weight loss is also shown on TGA diagrams related to CMC in three stages; when the temperature ranged from 25 to 140 °C, the first stage of weight loss was estimated to be 7.8%., which is due to the presence of moisture in the relevant scaffold because polysaccharides have a strong ability to absorb water (moisture) from have their surroundings. The second weight loss is 39% in the temperature range of 245 to 299 °C; that’s because of the loss of -COO groups in the sample, and the final demotion of 20% in the temperature range of 542 to 600 °C is due to the degradation of the polymer backbone 103 . Figure  8 d shows the TGA curves of scaffolds containing PVA/Alg. Again, according to the previous examples, the scaffold containing PVA and Alg shows three stages of weight loss; the first stage, below 110 °C, is related to the presence of physically absorbed water. At temperatures between 225 and 310 °C, the weight loss is about 48% due to the destruction of chains and side groups in both PVA and Alg polymers, and in the third stage of weight loss, the main chains are destroyed between 403 and 435 °C. At a temperature of 600 °C, almost all of the existing PVA decomposes and disappears, while alginate shows a 40% weight loss at this temperature. Of course, 15.6% of the material’s weight remains due to three times more PVA than Alg in the PVA and Alg scaffold. The thermal properties of polyvinyl alcohol and alginate increase during mixing 104 . In the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold, the same three main stages of weight loss are observed, with the difference that the presence of HAp increases the thermal stability of the scaffold. In the end, 24% of the scaffold’s weight remains at 600 °C. All three stages of weight loss are observed in the PVA/Alg/Hap/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Still, in this scaffold, the third stage of weight loss begins at a temperature of 420 °C, which shows the positive effect of CGF nanoparticles in increasing the thermal stability of the nanocomposite scaffold and the uniform broadcasting of these nanoparticles in the inside of the polymer matrix shows the scaffold. Figure  8 e shows the TGA diagram of scaffolds containing PVA and CMC. These scaffolds also show three stages of weight loss related to physically absorbed water, the degradation of polymer chains and side groups of the scaffold, and the degradation of main polymer chains. The weight loss with a high slope at around 400 °C in the PVA-CMC-HAp scaffold is related to the water absorbed in the HAp network 105 . Also, HAp particles in the polymer scaffold have increased its thermal stability 106 , 107 . As a result of the uniform distribution of CGF nanoparticles in the PVA-CMC-HAp-CGF scaffold, a weight loss with a lower slope is observed at the last stage. Also, the attendance of clay can increase the thermal stability of the scaffold. As observed, in both polymer matrices, the polymer composite’s thermal stability increased with the addition of additives, which may be caused by increasing the polymer matrix’s physical and chemical bond density. Physical bond density is the amount of non-electronic interactions between atoms, such as ion-based or volume-based interactions, and chemical bond density is related to the number of bonds, such as covalent or electron-sharing, among the atoms of the polymer matrix 28 . Furthermore, compared to the pure mixture, the nanocomposite has a lower tendency to decompose 104 . Biomineralization of scaffolds in SBF analysis As shown in Fig.  9 a–f, after immersing the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/Alg/HAp and PVA/Alg nanocomposite scaffolds in SBF solution for 14 days, SEM images and EDX patterns were obtained. As it is clear from Fig.  9 a–f, the hydroxyapatite layer is formed on the surface of scaffolds containing Alg and PVA. Following the immersion of the scaffold in SBF solution for 14 days, HAp is precipitated as unique spherical particles by accumulating high concentrations of Ca 2+  and PO 4 3-  and forming on the surface of the scaffold 108 . Alginate as a biopolymer shows good biomineralization; adding HAp to it also creates an organic–inorganic compound, similar to the bone structure, which increases the biomineralization of the resulting scaffold 109 . By adding HAp to the raw materials of the scaffold, there is a noticeable increase in the formation of apatite crystals in the sample; according to the theory, this phenomenon occurs when Ca 2+  ions are released from the scaffold structure and form an ionic bond with the SBF solution, which in turn leads to a more saturated concentration of Ca 2+  ions and a higher pH value for the SBF solution, and as a result, apatite crystallization increases on the scaffold surface 110 . GO material facilitates spontaneous osteogenic differentiation and improvement of the biomineralization process 23 , 111 . The negatively charged carboxyl groups in GO interact with the Ca 2+  cation in hydroxyapatite, thus facilitating hydroxyapatite formation. Also, scaffolds containing clay, using the nucleation site for hydroxyapatite deposition, show a high ability to induce the formation of hydroxyapatite because clay has a negative charge on its surface, which, like GO, can improve the hydroxyapatite precipitation on the surface of the scaffold 67 . As a result, according to the reasons explained, by adding CGF nanoparticles, the biomineralization of nanocomposite scaffolds increases. Figure  9 g–l shows mineralization on the surfaces of scaffolds containing CMC biopolymer. Adding CMC to a scaffold also increases its biomineralization; the higher the amount of CMC inside a scaffold, the higher the formation of HAp on its surface 112 , 113 . The carboxyl groups that make up CMC have a negative charge, and this causes them to interact with the calcium ions, which have a positive charge. To increase the adsorption of phosphate ions, calcium ions are bound to the carboxyl group in SBF, and this causes the adsorption of hydroxyapatite particles to grow on the nanocomposite scaffold that was prepared 114 . As seen in Fig.  9 g–l, both PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds showed good biomineralization, and there was an increase in biomineralization with the increase in CGF and HAp levels. Fig. 9 SEM images showing mineralization on the surface of scaffolds ( a – c ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF after 14 days of exposure in SBF solution, and EDX analysis results for ( d – f ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds during the biomineralization process, ( g – i ) SEM images of PVA/CMC, PVA/CMC/HAp, and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds after 14 days in SBF solution, and EDX analysis results for ( j – l ) PVA/CMC, PVA/CMC/HAp, and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds during biomineralization. The distribution of phosphorus and calcium elements in PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg nanocomposite scaffolds is shown in Fig.  10 . According to the results shown in this figure, as increasing CGF and HAp, the amount of mineralization has also increased, so the density of phosphorus and calcium elements has also increased. Apatite mineralization of bioactive substances is essential in tissue formation, growth, and maintenance. In addition, a mineral apatite layer can increase osteoblastic activity, which probably stimulates cell proliferation and activates cell differentiation by binding serum proteins and growth factors to the substrate 115 . Fig. 10 The distribution of phosphorus (P) on the surface of ( a – c ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds, as well as the distribution of calcium (Ca) in ( d – f ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds after immersing in SBF solution for 14 days. Figure  11  shows the distribution of calcium and phosphorus elements on the surfaces of scaffolds containing CMC. As you can see in the figure, the uniform dispensation of elements can be seen on the surfaces of the scaffolds. As a result, apatite is uniformly formed at the surface of the scaffolds. Also, compared to scaffolds containing Alg, scaffolds containing CMC showed denser distribution values of calcium and phosphorus elements, indicating more mineralization on scaffolds made of CMC biopolymer. Figure  11  also shows the distribution of calcium and phosphorus elements in the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, which, compared to the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, obtains a more homogeneous and denser distribution of phosphorus and Ca 2+  elements due to the distribution The homogeneity of the fillers inside the matrix contains CMC biopolymer and this indicates the absence of nanoparticle aggregation in both nanocomposite scaffolds. Fig. 11 Distribution of phosphorus element (P) on the surface of ( a – c ) PVA/CMC and PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds, and distribution of calcium element (Ca) on the surface of ( d – f ) PVA/CMC and PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds which were placed in SBF solution for 14 days. Cell viability and toxicity test in laboratory conditions One of the important features of scaffolds designed for tissue engineering is biocompatibility, which indicates that they are non-toxic to cells for in vivo applications. A toxicity test called MTT was performed to examine the effect of biocompatibility proliferation and cell survival on the scaffolds. As a control sample, no scaffolds were used in the culture, and the toxicity test was performed in 72 h. The cell viability results in the scaffolds using the MTT toxicity test are shown in Fig.  12 . When comparing the control sample to the existing scaffolds, it is evident that all the scaffolds exhibit greater cell viability than the control, suggesting that the prepared polymeric scaffolds are non-toxic. In addition to increasing mechanical properties, polymer composites can create a suitable environment for cell adhesion and growth. As shown in Fig.  12 a, the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds demonstrated the highest cell viability. This may be due to the added amount of Fe 3 O 4  and GO to the polymeric matrix of the porous nanocomposite scaffold. Different functional groups of the polymeric matrix also increase cell survival due to more excellent cell adhesion. Hence, the synergistic effect of Fe 3 O 4  and GO increases cell survival due to the cell compatibility nature of porous scaffolds 44 . One of the applications of GO in bone regeneration has been to construct a conductive bone scaffold to increase cell adhesion and provide a matrix for cell proliferation. The mechanical strength and tunable surface chemistry of GO have made it appear a promising platform for achieving the goal of cell proliferation 116 . Also, the CMC enhances the incorporation of hydrated media into the scaffolds to enhance cell adhesion without cytotoxicity 76 . The scaffolds’ high swelling ratio and heterogeneous porous structure enable efficient exchange of nutrients and waste materials, thereby enhancing cell growth 117 . According to the results of the swelling section, it was observed that the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold had the highest swelling rate, and it was also seen in the SEM section that this scaffold had heterogeneous porosity, so the highest amount of cell viability was also observed in this scaffold. According to the results of the toxicity test of the scaffolds using the MTT method, good cell viability was observed on the scaffolds. To observe the survival of cells on the prepared scaffolds, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, after cell culture using the method described in Section “ Assessment of toxicity and cell viability ”, was dried and subjected to SEM imaging and its SEM images are shown in Fig.  12 b–c. As shown in Fig.  12 b, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold has large and small pores in its structure, which is a suitable porous scaffold for bone tissue engineering. Also, Fig.  12 c shows the SEM image of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold after 72 h of cell culture using the MTT toxicity test, which shows that all the pores in the appearance have disappeared. This indicates that over the 72-h cell culture period, the cells have proliferated on the scaffold and populated all of its pores, which aligns with the findings in Fig.  11 , showing the highest cell growth and survival in the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Fig. 12 ( a ) The impact of scaffold biocompatibility on cell culture and cell viability using the MTT test after 72 h of cell culture, with SEM images of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold in ( b ) before and ( c ) after 72 h of cell culture.

Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis In the structure of HAp prepared from the chicken thigh in Fig.  1 a, the peak in the wave number range of 1090–1045 cm -1  indicates the vibrations of PO 4 3-  in the structure of HAp, and the vibration of -OH is observed in 3570 cm -1 39 , 40 . The 568 cm -1  and 600–640 cm -1  peaks correspond to the bending and stretching vibrations of PO 4 3-  ions 41 . Meanwhile, the vibrations at 1400 cm -1  indicate the presence of carbonate, which influences the bioactive characteristics of HAp. HAp forms peaks that contain cations of Ca 2+ , which are associated with HAp 42 . Figure  1 a shows a spectrum of graphene oxide with peaks at 2920 and 2800 cm -1 , which correspond to the peaks of the -CH 2  group in graphene oxide. 3400 cm -1  peak is related with the OH group, 1574 cm -1  peak with C=C bond, and 1711 cm -1  peak with C=O bond 43 , 44 . The 1160 and 1050 cm -1  peaks in the diagram above correspond to the vibrations of C-OH and C-O, respectively 45 , 46 . The FT-IR spectrum of Fe 3 O 4  material, shown in Fig.  1 a, features characteristic bands at 3430 cm -1  and 1611 cm -1  associated with hydroxyl groups. The 590 cm -1  peak represents Fe-O vibrations, while the 3421 and 1569 cm -1  peaks are also attributed to hydroxyl groups in the Fe 3 O 4  compound 47 , 48 . The 2920 and 2850 cm -1  peaks are also linked to C-H stretching 49 . The FT-IR spectrum of the clay is shown in Fig.  1 a, where the peak at 1040 cm -1  is assigned to Si vibrations in the Si-O band. In the 3634–3393 cm -1  region of the FTIR spectrum, there is a broad absorption peak assigned to the OH stretching vibration. Two peaks at 2920 and 2854 cm -1  have been assigned to compounds that contain linear aliphatic chains and are indicative of clay’s organic modification 18 . Absorption peaks at 525, 460, and 1470 cm -1  were identified, corresponding to the vibrations of Si-O-Al, Si-O-Mg, and carbonate (CaCO 3  or Mg(CO 3 ) 2 ), respectively 50 . In the FT-IR spectrum of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  (CGF) material, a peak at 12,920 cm -1  was observed, attributed to C-H vibrations in graphene oxide 51 . Additionally, the absorption peak at 1460 cm -1  is linked to the C-O bond in graphene oxide 52 , while the peak at 1040 cm -1  corresponds to silicon in Cloisite 30B-modified clay 18 . After placing Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles and graphene oxide in the clay structure, the intensity and range of the absorption peaks in the clay structure changed significantly, which can be caused by the interaction between the functional groups of the clay surface, the iron, and graphene oxide nanoparticles. In addition, absorption vibrations in the said magnetic structure composites originating from Fe-O vibrations have been seen, confirming the presence of Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles in the structure 52 . Fig. 1 FT-IR spectra ( a ) HAp, CGF, Cloisite 30B, Fe 3 O 4 , GO, ( b ) CMC, Alg and, PVA biopolymers, ( c ) PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp and PVA/Alg scaffolds, and ( d ) PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF, PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC nanocomposite scaffolds. The FT-IR spectrum of the alginate biopolymer, shown in Fig.  1 b, includes peaks at 2920, 883, and 12,109 cm -1 , corresponding to the -OH group 53 . Peaks at 1418 and 1627 cm -1  are associated with the symmetric stretching of the carboxyl group and the carboxylate group (C=O) 54 , while the peak at 1125 cm -1  relates to C-O and C-C bonds 55 . The FT-IR spectrum of carboxymethyl cellulose, also depicted in Fig.  1 b, displays a strong, broad absorption band at 13,439 cm -1  due to -OH group stretching. The peak at 2919 cm -1  corresponds to C-H stretching vibrations 56 , 57 , the strong band at 1609 cm -1  indicates the presence of the -COO -  group, and absorption bands at 11,423 and 1328 cm -1  are attributed to -CH 2  and -OH vibrations. The peak at 11,059 cm -1  is also linked to CH-O-CH 2  bond stretching 57 . The FT-IR spectrum of the scaffold, composed of polyvinyl alcohol and sodium alginate, is shown in Fig.  1 c, highlighting the scaffold’s structural features. In this spectrum, the peak at 3423 cm -1  corresponds to OH present in sodium alginate and PVA, and the peak at 1628 cm -1  is assigned to the presence of -COO -  in alginate. The 850 and 1108 cm -1  peaks can be related to C-C and C-O of alginate and PVA 53 , 58 , 59 . The 1431 and 2923 cm -1  peaks represent CH-OH and CH in PVA or OH of alginate, respectively 60 , 61 . All the peaks of both PVA and alginate biopolymers were also present in the scaffold of the combination of these two biopolymers, and they show a slight shift, which indicates the mixing of materials and the formation of a polymer blend. In the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold shown in Fig.  1 c, the new peak appearing at 1569 cm -1  is assigned to the presence of the -PO 4 3-  group. The peak in 1043 cm -1  also indicates the attendance of -PO 4 3-  in the scaffold. The peak at 609 cm -1  corresponds to the -OH group associated with hydroxyapatite, while the peak at 1425 cm -1  represents -CO 3 2 ⁻ related to HAp 35 . The presence of these new peaks in the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold, compared to the PVA/Alg scaffold, confirms the incorporation of HAp. In the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold, additional peaks have emerged alongside those from the previous scaffold. Peaks at 431 and 471 cm -1  are attributed to Si-O-Mg and Si-O-Al vibrations 50 , indicating the formation of Cloisite 30B clay bonds within the new scaffold structure. Vibrations around 571 cm -1  correspond to the Fe-O bond, while peaks at 1429 and 2935 cm -1  are associated with C-O and C-H vibrations in graphene oxide (GO), confirming the presence of polymers 52 . These observations verify the incorporation of CGF into the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Additionally, slight shifts in the PVA, Alg, and HAp peaks suggest effective integration of the materials within the scaffold. Since no new peaks appeared in the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold compared to the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold, it can be inferred that CGF was incorporated into the polymer matrix through physical interactions. Figure  1 d presents the FT-IR spectra of PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF, PVA/CMC/HAp, and PVA/CMC scaffolds. In the PVA/CMC scaffold spectrum, the broad peak at 3420 cm -1  corresponds to the -OH stretching vibrations of the hydroxyl groups in PVA and CMC. In contrast, the peak around 1330 cm -1  is associated with C-O vibrations of the acetyl groups in PVA 56 , 57 , 62 . The peak in the 1603 cm-1 region is associated with the symmetrical bending of the C-O bond in PVA and CMC 62 , 63 , and the C-O absorption band in the 1096 cm -1  region is due to the attendance of CMC and PVA. The 846, 1426, and 2938 cm -1  peaks show C-C, CH-OH, and CH in PVA and CMC, respectively 64 , 65 . The displacement of the peaks compared to the peak of the original samples shows the combination of materials and the formation of a polymer blend. It is expected that hydrogen bonding will occur between PVA and CMC, and these forces occur in the bending region of OH and -COO- functional group 66 . Comparing the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold with that of the PVA/CMC scaffold reveals that the peak at 1603 cm -1  becomes sharper, and a new peak appears around 570 cm -1 , corresponding to the bending and stretching vibrations of the -PO 4 3 ⁻ group 41 . These peaks confirm the presence of HAp in the scaffold. Furthermore, the FT-IR spectrum of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold shows the emergence of a new peak at 496 cm -1 , attributed to the vibrations of Si bonds in Cloisite 30B clay, indicating the incorporation of CGF into the final PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold 50 . The mixing of CGF into the polymer matrix of this nanocomposite scaffold was achieved through physical interactions.

X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis XRD analysis was done to identify the crystal structure of the samples, including the different phases of the crystal regions. In Fig.  2 a, the XRD spectrum shows the structure of natural HAp, in which peaks with different intensities appear and shows that the natural HAp powder has a crystalline structure. The crystalline phases “102” and “002” of hydroxyapatite were identified at 2θ angles of 26.28 and 26.02°, respectively 42 . Also, other peaks of crystalline phases of this material include “211”, “112”, “300”, “202”, “310”, “222”, “312”, “213”, “321”, “410”, “402”, and “004” respectively in the values of the 2θ angles 31.9, 23.3, 33.06, 34.26, 39.9, 46.8, 48.2, 49.6, 50.6, and 51.4. 52.2 and 53.3° are visible 41 . The index peak of GO material is created at the 2θ angle of 12.3° 44 , 67 , which indicates the “001” crystal phase 68 . In the XRD spectrum of Fe 3 O 4 , the peak at a 2θ angle of 30° corresponds to the crystal phase “220”, while peaks at 2θ angles of 37.7, 54.6, 57.16, 74.2, 63.3, and 42.8° represent the crystal phases “311”, “422”, “511”, “533”, “440”, and '“400”, respectively 56 . The XRD spectrum of Cloisite 30B clay, the characteristic peak at a 2θ angle of 5.02°, corresponds to the ‘001’ crystalline phase 69 , 70 . There are peaks in the structure of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  magnetic composite with different intensities, and these peaks match those seen in the structure of the components. This indicates that the components are correctly interrelated in the composite structure. In the composite structure, the peaks observed at 2θ angles of 63.1, 53.8, and 35.7° correspond to Fe 3 O 4  particles, while the peak at 11.84° is associated with graphene oxide. The peak at the 2θ angle of 6° is related to the attendance of Cloisite 30B clay in the composite. In the XRD spectrum of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  material, the peak of the GO index is significantly weakened, which is caused by the separation of the GO layers and the placement of Fe 3 O 4  particles between its layers, which indicates the successful preparation of the C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4  composite 52 . Also, the peak indicating Cloisite 30B clay has weakened, suggesting that its layers are separated. The XRD spectrum of alginate can be seen in Fig.  2 b. Alginate index peaks have appeared in the 2θ range of 19.08, 32.2, 33.9, 36, and 48.84° 71 , 72 . The X-ray diffraction of CMC biopolymer is shown in Fig.  2 b, and the main diffraction of this material is observed at 2θ of 20.04° 63 . As can be seen in this figure, alginate polymer has sharper peaks than CMC polymer. Figure  2 c shows the XRD spectrum of PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg scaffolds. In the spectrum of the PVA/Alg scaffold, the index peak appeared in this scaffold in the range of 2θ of 19.8°; both biopolymers from PVA and Alg were found to be present in the scaffold structure. Also, the peaks at 2θ of 22.56, 31.8, 33.86, and 46.2° are due to alginate biopolymer 63 , 71 . By comparing the peak of the PVA/Alg scaffold with the peaks of PVA and Alg, it can be seen that the peaks of the polymer blend scaffold have been slightly shifted and reduced in intensity compared to the original samples, which shows that the biopolymers of this scaffold have interacted well and a suitable polymer blend has been created. XRD spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold is shown in Fig.  2 b. By comparing this spectrum with the spectrum of the PVA/Alg scaffold, it can be seen that the peak at the 2θ angle of 31.8° has become much sharper, which shows the effective presence of HAp in the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold. Also, a new peak has been created at the 2θ angle of 49.5°, which was absent in the PVA/Alg scaffold 73 . In the XRD spectrum of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, a new peak at a 2θ angle of 5° is observed, indicating the presence of Cloisite 30B clay 69 . Additionally, the peak at 11.4° corresponds to graphene oxide (GO) 44 , while the peaks at 42.02, 57.9, 63.4, and 74.8° are associated with the Fe 3 O 4  crystalline structure 74 . These peaks confirm the inclusion of CGF material components in the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. However, the slight weakening of the Cloisite 30B clay peak suggests the formation of an exfoliated structure within the nanocomposite scaffold. The XRD spectra of the PVA and CMC scaffolds are shown in Fig.  2 d. The peak of the PVA/CMC scaffold appears at 19.78°, which confirms the presence of both PVA and CMC polymers, but the peak that appears is broader than the PVA and CMC peaks and also shifts concerning both materials, which indicates a strong interaction between PVA and CMC. In the XRD spectrum of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold, the peaks that appear at 08.32, 8.33, 9.46, 56.40, and 5.49° are related to the presence and effect of hydroxyapatite in the scaffold 39 . By comparing the XRD spectra of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold with the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold, it is observed that the small peak that appears at 5° is affected by the presence of C30B, and the peak at 8.11° is related to GO. These newly formed peaks confirm the presence of CGF in the scaffold, but compared to CGF, the peak corresponding to Cloisite 30B has almost disappeared, indicating the separation of the clay layers and the formation of the exfoliated structure of the nanocomposite. Fig. 2 XRD diagrams of ( a ) HAp, CGF, C30B, Fe 3 O 4 , and GO, ( b ) CMC, ALg, and PVA biopolymers, ( c ) PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp and PVA/Alg scaffolds, and d) PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF, PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC scaffolds.

Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) analysis The morphological properties of HAp obtained from the femur of chicken, modified clay, and synthesized scaffolds have been evaluated using FESEM analysis and EDX analysis. As seen in Fig.  3 a, the produced HAp has a crystalline structure. In Fig.  3 b, as seen in the EDX analysis of HAp, the elements Mg, Na, and Zn are observed in addition to the main elements P and Ca. Magnesium, sodium, and zinc elements in the structure of hydroxyapatite can play a very important role in influencing its biological function. Magnesium impurities in HAp can promote osteoblast growth and multiplication in bone tissue. Zinc incorporation into the HAp structure is highly beneficial for bone regeneration, as it promotes the proliferation of osteoclasts and osteoblasts while enhancing bone formation. The attendance of Na improves the biocompatibility and cellular characteristics of HAp 41 . As it is clear from the SEM images and EDX analysis of clay modified with Fe 3 O 4  and GO in Fig.  3 c–d, clay and graphene oxide have a layered structure, and after adding and changing it with compounds containing Fe (II) and Fe (III), Fe 3 O 4  nanoparticles with different sizes and spherical morphology have been created on the clay surface. Therefore, Cloisite 30B particles, graphene oxide, and Fe 3 O 4  have adequate and proper interaction, and the peaks belonging to O, Fe, Si, and C elements show the modification of clay with graphene oxide and Fe 3 O 4 . Fig. 3 ( a ) SEM image of hydroxyapatite prepared from the chicken femur, ( b ) EDX analysis results of natural hydroxyapatite, ( c ) SEM image of C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 , and ( d ) EDX analysis result of C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 . Alginate biopolymer is suitable for hard tissue engineering, has no toxic properties, and is biocompatible 75 . Figure  4 a shows the scaffold morphology produced from the PVA/Alg polymer blend. This scaffold has a proper porosity distribution, and large and small porosity can be seen in the image. Figure  4 b illustrates the morphology of the PVA/Alg/HAp polymer scaffold, showing that adding HAp slightly reduces its porosity. The SEM image of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold in Fig.  4 c reveals a further reduction in porosity and a denser structure attributed to the inclusion of GO nanoparticles and Cloisite 30B clay. Additionally, CMC, a biopolymer with high water absorption and biodegradable bonds, plays a role in adjusting the scaffold’s porous structure and enhancing its swelling capacity 76 . Figure  4 d displays the morphology of the PVA/CMC blend scaffold. As is apparent in the image, this scaffold has a perfect distribution of porosity, which is very suitable for the stage of cell culture and bone tissue regeneration. Large and small pores are seen simultaneously in this scaffold. CMC biopolymer in a hydrogel blend with PVA has increased the pore size of scaffold pores. The presence of CMC in the hydrogel absorbs water, resulting in the formation of more ice crystals in the scaffolds, and after sublimation, larger voids are formed in the scaffold 76 . Figure  4 e also shows the morphology of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold, whose porosity has been reduced due to the addition of hydroxyapatite. Finally, Fig.  4 f shows the scaffold morphology from adding CGF nanoparticles to the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold. The increase of these nanoparticles causes a slight reduction in the porosity of the nanocomposite scaffold, and a suitable distribution of the size of the pores can be seen. By comparing the prepared PVA/Alg and PVA/CMC scaffolds, it can be seen that the porosity distribution in the PVA/CMC scaffold is higher, which shows the suitability of this scaffold for cell growth and proliferation. Porosity is one of the important factors in determining mechanical properties. Reducing the porosity significantly enhanced the scaffold’s mechanical properties 77 , 78 . Therefore, the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds have higher mechanical properties than those without CGF nanoparticles. As stated in the XRD analysis, the alginate polymer had sharper peaks than the CMC polymer, and the composites containing the alginate biopolymer also showed sharper peaks than the scaffolds containing the CMC biopolymer, which caused more pores in the scaffolds containing alginate, which can also be seen in the SEM images in Fig.  4 . Fig. 4 SEM images of various scaffolds: ( a ) PVA/Alg, ( b ) PVA/Alg/HAp, ( c ) PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, ( d ) PVA/CMC, ( e ) PVA/CMC/HAp, and ( f ) PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF. One of the essential parameters of an ideal scaffold is its porosity so that nutrients and oxygen can easily pass through its pores and reach the cells, and waste products can pass through them and be excreted. The size of the pores should not be so large that they impair mechanical strength nor so small that they become blocked by cells and prevent the penetration of cells and vessels into the interior of the scaffold. Figure  5 a–c shows the microstructure and dot-mapping of PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffolds. Figure  5 a shows the scaffold morphology with large pores; this scaffold comprises only two polymers: PVA and Alg. Figure  5 b presents the morphology of the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold. As it is clear by comparing this scaffold with the scaffold of the previous form, the scaffold’s porosity is reduced, and the accumulation of white material indicates hydroxyapatite particles on the surface of this polymer scaffold. Also, this figure shows the distribution of the elements from the EDX analysis in the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold. Ca (green dots) and P (red dots) elements are seen due to the attendance of HAp in the scaffold, which shows a uniform distribution of these elements in the scaffolding. Figure  5 c shows the microstructure of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffold, which is still porous. However, it has relatively low porosity, and HAp and CGF nanoparticles are visible. In this figure, the distribution of various elements in the microstructure of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold can be seen, where Fe and Si elements indicate the attendance of CGF nanomaterial in the scaffold, and P and Ca elements indicate the HAp material, which has a uniform distribution. Fig. 5 ( a – c ) SEM with dot-mapping images of the PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF scaffolds. Figure  6  shows the SEM microstructure and dot-mapping of scaffolds containing PVA and CMC biopolymers. Figure  6 a shows the structure and the distribution of elements of the PVA/CMC scaffold, and Fig.  6 b shows distribution of elements of the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold. As it is clear from these figures, this scaffold appeared to have less porosity than the previous scaffold due to the presence of HAp. Also, in the distribution of elements in this scaffold presented in these figures, the presence of Ca and P elements was proof of the presence of HAp in this scaffold. The uniform distribution of calcium and phosphorus elements is also observed, showing the homogenous HAp dispersion in the scaffold. The last SEM image is the microstructure of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold. The uniform distribution of P, Ca, Fe, and Si elements in this scaffold in Fig.  6 c indicates the effective presence of CGF and HAp nanoparticles and their homogeneous distribution within the polymer matrix of this scaffold. As seen in Fig.  6 b and c, there is no accumulation of nanomaterials in these scaffolds, which indicates a very homogeneous distribution of HAp particles and CGF nanoparticles in the scaffolds, which can cause higher mechanical properties due to the reduction of stress concentration points. Also, in this scaffold, due to the homogeneous distribution of HAp particles in its polymer matrix, we will witness more and more homogeneous biomineralization. Fig. 6 ( a – c ) SEM microstructures and dot-mapping results of PVA/CMC, PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffolds.

Compariosn of the compressive strength, porosity, and swelling of scaffold samples A scaffold ideal for bone tissue engineering must have appropriate mechanical properties to support tissue growth when subjected to mechanical loading 79 . GO-containing scaffolds have excellent thermal and mechanical properties because GO is well dispersed in the polymer scaffold 80 , 81 . The wrinkled surface of GO sheets leads to high entanglement and adhesion between the polymer matrix and GO 82 . The presence of GO in nanocomposites significantly improves their tensile strength, Young’s modulus, and compressive strength. Improving the nanocomposite’s mechanical properties can be attributed to the interaction between the carboxyl groups in GO and the groups in polysaccharides 83 . Therefore, the weak mechanical properties of the polymer matrix can be increased by adding GO 84 . Hydrogen bonding between the hydroxyl and carbonyl groups on the GO sheets and the oxygen-containing functional groups of the polymer chains is possible, increasing the scaffold’s mechanical strength. The presence of GO/Fe 3 O 4  in the scaffold can improve the strain and stress of the scaffold 52 . The presence of graphene oxide in the nanocomposite enables it to absorb more energy before failure, enhance toughness, and lead to improved structural integrity 85 . Increased pore wall thickness and reduced porosity improve the mechanical properties of scaffolds, as Clay reduces porosity 86 . Clay’s presence causes the polymer scaffold’s cell pores to collapse. After the complete collapse of the cell pores, the walls of the opposite cell pores come into contact with each other and compress the solid more, creating a denser region, which ultimately addition the scaffold’s mechanical properties 87 . The results show that the incorporation of C30B/GO/Fe 3 O 4 , with the combined presence of graphene oxide, Clay, and Fe 3 O 4 , has enhanced the compressive strength of the scaffold. Figure  7 a–b shows the maximum compressive strength values of the scaffolds containing Alg and CMC biopolymers, and based on these results, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold with 12 MPa has the highest mechanical strength. The SEM images from this scaffold showed it had the least porosity. CGF and HAp nanoparticles were well distributed in this scaffold, which reduced the stress concentration points to the lowest value and increased the scaffold’s mechanical properties. The maximum compressive strength values reported for scaffold CS-Gel/nHA-PANi were 5.06 MPa 88 , for scaffold BC-g(Fe3O4/GO) were 11.6 MPa 52 , and for scaffold PLA/HA, about 12 MPa were obtained 89 . For the scaffold, CS-Gel/nHA/MMT, 5.11 MPa were obtained 86 . These cases indicate that the maximum compressive strength value obtained in this study is a suitable number compared to similar works and is also within the range of cancellous bone (2–20 MPa). Fig. 7 ( a – b ) Results of compressive strength, ( c – d ) porosity, and ( e – f ) percentage of water absorption in different prepared scaffolds. Porosity is an important factor when using polymer scaffolds to enhance cell culture. Porosity is helpful for blood vessels and cell growth. Still, if there are many macropores in the scaffold, it will cause a noticeable drop in its mechanical properties. Therefore, choosing a scaffold with a suitable ratio of large and small pores is essential for bone tissue regeneration 90 . For bone defects, polymer scaffolds with a porosity higher than 80% are unsuitable 91 , 92 . According to the results shown in Fig.  7 c–d, the porosity of the final PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffolds was 79 and 72%, respectively, which is to provide an opportunity for nutrient transport and cell migration is sufficient 84 . As stated in the previous section, the attendance of clay in the scaffold reduces its porosity 93 . It is possible to occupy the free -OH groups in the polymer matrix by adding GO, which increases the hydrophobicity of the nanocomposite scaffold and, as a result, reduces the percentage of porosity 94 . The control of the swelling behavior of the scaffolds, just like the control of the porosity parameter, is also of utmost importance if the scaffolds are to maintain their integrity in interaction with biological fluids. Thus, the swelling of the scaffolds can be attributed to the properties inherent in the constituent materials, which are responsible for causing the swelling. This allows control of swelling ability at the level of material selection 11 . The overall porosity and pore size are increased by swelling, resulting in the maximum possible surface area of the scaffold. When a sample shows a high degree of swelling, it can accept the maximum cell injection into the scaffold and create the maximum cell growth on the surface of the scaffold 95 . A proper interaction between the scaffold and the physiological fluid is essential for initial cell attachment, ultimately leading to better cell proliferation 91 . As shown in Fig.  7 e–f, the most swelling was obtained in CMC scaffolds. Considering that only the type of biopolymer used was different in the prepared polymer scaffolds, the results of Fig.  7 e–f show that the scaffold containing alginate biopolymer has the lowest amount of water absorption, and the scaffold containing CMC achieves higher swelling. CMC has exceptional water absorption properties, the bonds of which are biodegradable, and is derived from carbohydrate sources 76 ; therefore, due to its very hydrophilic nature, it increases the size of scaffold pores in a hydrogel-containing PVA, which, in turn, increases the amount of scaffold swelling. In PVA/CMC scaffolds, negatively charged carbonyl groups (CH 2 COO - ) from CMC and uncharged carboxyl groups on PVA are expected to contribute to the hydrophilicity of the scaffolds 76 . The incorporation of HAp reduces the hydrophilicity of the scaffolds through the binding of calcium and phosphate with the –COOH and –NH 2  groups of the polymers. Also, due to the addition of clay, the interaction between polymer macromolecules and water molecules is limited, reducing the water content absorbed by the scaffold. Scaffolds containing GO can absorb more water than scaffolds containing only polymer. High water absorption in scaffolds containing GO is due to many oxygen functional groups on GO; this process forms a hydrogen bond between the water and the GO. Incorporation of GO into polymer scaffolds is desirable to improve water absorption, which can help the absorption of drugs or nutrients into porous scaffolds 84 . The swelling values obtained in this study for scaffolds PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF are 2056 and 2412, respectively, compared to scaffold gelatin/chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol/nano-hydroxyapatite, where the swelling value was reported to be 700 90  which is a suitable value for bone substitute tissue. For scaffold chitosan/cellulose nanocrystals/halloysite nanotubes, the swelling value is reported to be 2200 91 .

Biodegradation measurements, and Thermogravimetric (TGA) analysis The disintegration behavior of a scaffold is an essential factor in achieving a successful regeneration process, as it must match the rate of tissue formation. Scaffold samples must be stable enough to withstand regeneration 67 . Polyvinyl alcohol is a synthetic biodegradable polymer 96 . When composite scaffolds are soaked in PBS solution, they hydrolyze chemical bonds within them, causing weight loss due to the hydrolysis 90 . As a function of the time the scaffolds were submerged in PBS solution, the weight loss of the scaffolds was determined, as shown in Fig.  8 a–b. As shown in this figure, in the first week, all scaffolds had a higher degradation rate, but after that, their degradation rate decreased. PBS medium reduces the weight of the scaffolds rapidly since physical and hydrogen bonds mainly connect them. HAp synthesized from chicken femur is crystalline, as shown in the results of SEM analysis. This high crystallization of HAp can reduce its degradation kinetics, and therefore, HAp can help maintain the scaffold’s stability 97 . Adding HAp to the polymer scaffold containing polysaccharides reduces the buffer access to the sites of the polysaccharide molecules, which reduces the degradation of the scaffold 88 . Therefore, scaffolds made of different biopolymers containing HAp show low degradation 11 . The results for the PVA/CMC/HAp scaffold show that adding HAp has reduced the degradation rate of the scaffold. The ions released from HAp can reduce the rate of the degradation process and thus increase the bioactivity of the polymer scaffold 98 . Adding clay to the nanocomposite scaffold improves the degradation rate of the scaffold, and increasing the hydrophilicity of the scaffolds containing clay increases the diffusion of PBS inside the scaffolds and causes hydrolytic degradation 20 . Adding clay modified with Fe 3 O 4  and GO reduces the degradation rate of the final scaffold. The clay plates form a strong interaction with the macromolecular chains of the biopolymer scaffold, which causes the hydrophilic groups to be consumed and the amount of solvent absorption to decrease, which protects the macromolecules from the hydrolysis process. Also, clay increases the stability of the polymer network 67 . Adding GO reduces the scaffold degradation rate 92 , 99 . The decrease in degradation of the polymer scaffold when adding GO is probably because GO sheets prevent the penetration of destructive substances in the phosphate buffer solution. In addition, the polymer matrix can have a more regular arrangement in the presence of GO, which is desirable for less penetration of destructive substances 99 . The increase of Fe 3 O 4 /GO reduces the degradation of the porous scaffold 52 . Alginate biopolymer has high biodegradability and is used as a drug and growth factor carrier 13 . Therefore, the PVA/Alg scaffold showed high biodegradability, which was reduced by adding HAp and CGF; however, for the final PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, the biodegradation rate is in the appropriate range. Because CMC biopolymer has high water absorption ability and suitable biodegradable links 76 , the highest degradation was obtained in the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Fig. 8 ( a – b ) Degradation amount of scaffolds containing Alg and CMC under laboratory conditions in PBS in periods of 7, 14, and 21 days, ( c – e ) TGA curves of polymer and nanocomposite scaffolds. Thermograms of PVA, CMC, and Alg polymer materials are given in Fig.  8 c. The alginate thermogram shows that alginate decomposition started at around 25 °C, followed by weight loss due to the degrading of alginate chains and its monomers at 203 and 552 °C, respectively 100 , 101 . In the 203 to 256 °C temperature range, the weight loss is due to removing the -COO groups. The residue consists of elements that decompose above 900 °C 100 . In the TGA diagram related to polyvinyl alcohol in Fig.  8 c, the weight loss below 100 °C is related to removing physically absorbed water. Due to this polymer’s ease of absorption, a relatively significant amount of water has been absorbed. The main weight loss occurs in the second stage within the 211 to 331 °C temperature range, associated with the breakdown of PVA polymer side chains. The third stage of weight loss occurs between 400 and 490 °C, corresponding to the degradation of the polymer’s main chains 102 . Weight loss is also shown on TGA diagrams related to CMC in three stages; when the temperature ranged from 25 to 140 °C, the first stage of weight loss was estimated to be 7.8%., which is due to the presence of moisture in the relevant scaffold because polysaccharides have a strong ability to absorb water (moisture) from have their surroundings. The second weight loss is 39% in the temperature range of 245 to 299 °C; that’s because of the loss of -COO groups in the sample, and the final demotion of 20% in the temperature range of 542 to 600 °C is due to the degradation of the polymer backbone 103 . Figure  8 d shows the TGA curves of scaffolds containing PVA/Alg. Again, according to the previous examples, the scaffold containing PVA and Alg shows three stages of weight loss; the first stage, below 110 °C, is related to the presence of physically absorbed water. At temperatures between 225 and 310 °C, the weight loss is about 48% due to the destruction of chains and side groups in both PVA and Alg polymers, and in the third stage of weight loss, the main chains are destroyed between 403 and 435 °C. At a temperature of 600 °C, almost all of the existing PVA decomposes and disappears, while alginate shows a 40% weight loss at this temperature. Of course, 15.6% of the material’s weight remains due to three times more PVA than Alg in the PVA and Alg scaffold. The thermal properties of polyvinyl alcohol and alginate increase during mixing 104 . In the PVA/Alg/HAp scaffold, the same three main stages of weight loss are observed, with the difference that the presence of HAp increases the thermal stability of the scaffold. In the end, 24% of the scaffold’s weight remains at 600 °C. All three stages of weight loss are observed in the PVA/Alg/Hap/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Still, in this scaffold, the third stage of weight loss begins at a temperature of 420 °C, which shows the positive effect of CGF nanoparticles in increasing the thermal stability of the nanocomposite scaffold and the uniform broadcasting of these nanoparticles in the inside of the polymer matrix shows the scaffold. Figure  8 e shows the TGA diagram of scaffolds containing PVA and CMC. These scaffolds also show three stages of weight loss related to physically absorbed water, the degradation of polymer chains and side groups of the scaffold, and the degradation of main polymer chains. The weight loss with a high slope at around 400 °C in the PVA-CMC-HAp scaffold is related to the water absorbed in the HAp network 105 . Also, HAp particles in the polymer scaffold have increased its thermal stability 106 , 107 . As a result of the uniform distribution of CGF nanoparticles in the PVA-CMC-HAp-CGF scaffold, a weight loss with a lower slope is observed at the last stage. Also, the attendance of clay can increase the thermal stability of the scaffold. As observed, in both polymer matrices, the polymer composite’s thermal stability increased with the addition of additives, which may be caused by increasing the polymer matrix’s physical and chemical bond density. Physical bond density is the amount of non-electronic interactions between atoms, such as ion-based or volume-based interactions, and chemical bond density is related to the number of bonds, such as covalent or electron-sharing, among the atoms of the polymer matrix 28 . Furthermore, compared to the pure mixture, the nanocomposite has a lower tendency to decompose 104 .

Biomineralization of scaffolds in SBF analysis As shown in Fig.  9 a–f, after immersing the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/Alg/HAp and PVA/Alg nanocomposite scaffolds in SBF solution for 14 days, SEM images and EDX patterns were obtained. As it is clear from Fig.  9 a–f, the hydroxyapatite layer is formed on the surface of scaffolds containing Alg and PVA. Following the immersion of the scaffold in SBF solution for 14 days, HAp is precipitated as unique spherical particles by accumulating high concentrations of Ca 2+  and PO 4 3-  and forming on the surface of the scaffold 108 . Alginate as a biopolymer shows good biomineralization; adding HAp to it also creates an organic–inorganic compound, similar to the bone structure, which increases the biomineralization of the resulting scaffold 109 . By adding HAp to the raw materials of the scaffold, there is a noticeable increase in the formation of apatite crystals in the sample; according to the theory, this phenomenon occurs when Ca 2+  ions are released from the scaffold structure and form an ionic bond with the SBF solution, which in turn leads to a more saturated concentration of Ca 2+  ions and a higher pH value for the SBF solution, and as a result, apatite crystallization increases on the scaffold surface 110 . GO material facilitates spontaneous osteogenic differentiation and improvement of the biomineralization process 23 , 111 . The negatively charged carboxyl groups in GO interact with the Ca 2+  cation in hydroxyapatite, thus facilitating hydroxyapatite formation. Also, scaffolds containing clay, using the nucleation site for hydroxyapatite deposition, show a high ability to induce the formation of hydroxyapatite because clay has a negative charge on its surface, which, like GO, can improve the hydroxyapatite precipitation on the surface of the scaffold 67 . As a result, according to the reasons explained, by adding CGF nanoparticles, the biomineralization of nanocomposite scaffolds increases. Figure  9 g–l shows mineralization on the surfaces of scaffolds containing CMC biopolymer. Adding CMC to a scaffold also increases its biomineralization; the higher the amount of CMC inside a scaffold, the higher the formation of HAp on its surface 112 , 113 . The carboxyl groups that make up CMC have a negative charge, and this causes them to interact with the calcium ions, which have a positive charge. To increase the adsorption of phosphate ions, calcium ions are bound to the carboxyl group in SBF, and this causes the adsorption of hydroxyapatite particles to grow on the nanocomposite scaffold that was prepared 114 . As seen in Fig.  9 g–l, both PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds showed good biomineralization, and there was an increase in biomineralization with the increase in CGF and HAp levels. Fig. 9 SEM images showing mineralization on the surface of scaffolds ( a – c ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF after 14 days of exposure in SBF solution, and EDX analysis results for ( d – f ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds during the biomineralization process, ( g – i ) SEM images of PVA/CMC, PVA/CMC/HAp, and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds after 14 days in SBF solution, and EDX analysis results for ( j – l ) PVA/CMC, PVA/CMC/HAp, and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds during biomineralization. The distribution of phosphorus and calcium elements in PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg nanocomposite scaffolds is shown in Fig.  10 . According to the results shown in this figure, as increasing CGF and HAp, the amount of mineralization has also increased, so the density of phosphorus and calcium elements has also increased. Apatite mineralization of bioactive substances is essential in tissue formation, growth, and maintenance. In addition, a mineral apatite layer can increase osteoblastic activity, which probably stimulates cell proliferation and activates cell differentiation by binding serum proteins and growth factors to the substrate 115 . Fig. 10 The distribution of phosphorus (P) on the surface of ( a – c ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds, as well as the distribution of calcium (Ca) in ( d – f ) PVA/Alg, PVA/Alg/HAp, and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds after immersing in SBF solution for 14 days. Figure  11  shows the distribution of calcium and phosphorus elements on the surfaces of scaffolds containing CMC. As you can see in the figure, the uniform dispensation of elements can be seen on the surfaces of the scaffolds. As a result, apatite is uniformly formed at the surface of the scaffolds. Also, compared to scaffolds containing Alg, scaffolds containing CMC showed denser distribution values of calcium and phosphorus elements, indicating more mineralization on scaffolds made of CMC biopolymer. Figure  11  also shows the distribution of calcium and phosphorus elements in the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, which, compared to the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF, obtains a more homogeneous and denser distribution of phosphorus and Ca 2+  elements due to the distribution The homogeneity of the fillers inside the matrix contains CMC biopolymer and this indicates the absence of nanoparticle aggregation in both nanocomposite scaffolds. Fig. 11 Distribution of phosphorus element (P) on the surface of ( a – c ) PVA/CMC and PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds, and distribution of calcium element (Ca) on the surface of ( d – f ) PVA/CMC and PVA/CMC/HAp and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds which were placed in SBF solution for 14 days.

Cell viability and toxicity test in laboratory conditions One of the important features of scaffolds designed for tissue engineering is biocompatibility, which indicates that they are non-toxic to cells for in vivo applications. A toxicity test called MTT was performed to examine the effect of biocompatibility proliferation and cell survival on the scaffolds. As a control sample, no scaffolds were used in the culture, and the toxicity test was performed in 72 h. The cell viability results in the scaffolds using the MTT toxicity test are shown in Fig.  12 . When comparing the control sample to the existing scaffolds, it is evident that all the scaffolds exhibit greater cell viability than the control, suggesting that the prepared polymeric scaffolds are non-toxic. In addition to increasing mechanical properties, polymer composites can create a suitable environment for cell adhesion and growth. As shown in Fig.  12 a, the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffolds demonstrated the highest cell viability. This may be due to the added amount of Fe 3 O 4  and GO to the polymeric matrix of the porous nanocomposite scaffold. Different functional groups of the polymeric matrix also increase cell survival due to more excellent cell adhesion. Hence, the synergistic effect of Fe 3 O 4  and GO increases cell survival due to the cell compatibility nature of porous scaffolds 44 . One of the applications of GO in bone regeneration has been to construct a conductive bone scaffold to increase cell adhesion and provide a matrix for cell proliferation. The mechanical strength and tunable surface chemistry of GO have made it appear a promising platform for achieving the goal of cell proliferation 116 . Also, the CMC enhances the incorporation of hydrated media into the scaffolds to enhance cell adhesion without cytotoxicity 76 . The scaffolds’ high swelling ratio and heterogeneous porous structure enable efficient exchange of nutrients and waste materials, thereby enhancing cell growth 117 . According to the results of the swelling section, it was observed that the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold had the highest swelling rate, and it was also seen in the SEM section that this scaffold had heterogeneous porosity, so the highest amount of cell viability was also observed in this scaffold. According to the results of the toxicity test of the scaffolds using the MTT method, good cell viability was observed on the scaffolds. To observe the survival of cells on the prepared scaffolds, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, after cell culture using the method described in Section “ Assessment of toxicity and cell viability ”, was dried and subjected to SEM imaging and its SEM images are shown in Fig.  12 b–c. As shown in Fig.  12 b, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold has large and small pores in its structure, which is a suitable porous scaffold for bone tissue engineering. Also, Fig.  12 c shows the SEM image of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold after 72 h of cell culture using the MTT toxicity test, which shows that all the pores in the appearance have disappeared. This indicates that over the 72-h cell culture period, the cells have proliferated on the scaffold and populated all of its pores, which aligns with the findings in Fig.  11 , showing the highest cell growth and survival in the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold. Fig. 12 ( a ) The impact of scaffold biocompatibility on cell culture and cell viability using the MTT test after 72 h of cell culture, with SEM images of the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold in ( b ) before and ( c ) after 72 h of cell culture.

Conclusion Nanocomposite scaffolds composed of natural biopolymers and additives were fabricated using freeze-drying. The optimal formulation incorporated 10 wt.% hydroxyapatite (HAp) and 2 wt. % modified magnetic clay (CGF) as fillers. The characterization results confirmed the development of a porous polymer matrix with desirable mechanical and physical properties. Among the tested scaffolds, PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF and PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF emerged as optimal candidates, demonstrating compressive strengths of 12 MPa and 8.1 MPa, and porosities of 72% and 79%, respectively, which fall within acceptable ranges for cancellous bone applications. Adding CGF to the polymer scaffold, a combination of clay modified with Fe 3 O 4  and graphene oxide, increased the compressive strength of the scaffold. Adding HAp and CGF enhanced the thermal stability of the polymer composites. Both scaffolds exhibited suitable biodegradability and swelling properties for bone tissue engineering, with the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffold showing a biodegradation rate of 43% over 21 days. The swelling ratios of the PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffolds were 1586% and 1860%, respectively. Furthermore, good biomineralization was observed in simulated body fluid (SBF). The presence of Fe 3 O 4  in the CGF additive had a positive effect on increasing cell culture in polymer scaffolds. Cell culture studies, including MTT toxicity tests over 72 h, revealed favorable cell viability, with optical density (OD) values of 1.451 and 1.483 for PVA/Alg/HAp/CGF and PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF scaffolds. Overall, the PVA/CMC/HAp/CGF nanocomposite scaffold, containing optimal additive concentrations, demonstrates significant potential as an alternative material for bone tissue engineering applications.
